% Section XI of the tariff (textiles and textile articles), as far as Chapters
% 61 (knitted or crocheted apparel) and 62 (apparel not knitted) need it: the
% textile material a garment is of (Section XI note 2 and subheading note 2),
% the vocabulary in which customs rulings describe garments and clothing
% accessories, and the notes that Chapters 61 and 62 share — what a suit, an
% ensemble, a shirt, a blouse, a babies' garment is, and the closure rule for
% men's and women's garments. A library: chapter_61.le and chapter_62.le
% include it, and it includes gri.le.
%
% Text: HTSUS Revision 18 (2026), Section XI notes 2 and 14 and subheading
% note 2, Chapter 61 notes 3, 4, 6, 7, 9 and Chapter 62 notes 3, 4, 5, 7, 9
% (https://hts.usitc.gov/). The notes quoted are the WCO text, identical in the
% EU Combined Nomenclature. Where the two chapters have the same note, the rule
% cites Chapter 61's and a comment says so.
%
% HOW A GOOD IS DESCRIBED. A ruling names the article ("a men's jacket", "a
% pair of women's trousers", "ankle socks"): that is its KIND ("the kind of
% style 345H115C is jacket"), and each kind falls in a FAMILY that the headings
% name ("jacket falls in the family outerwear"). Tops — shirts, blouses,
% T-shirts, tank tops, sweaters, sweatshirts, cardigans, knit vests — are
% "upper body garments" and are told apart by what the ruling observes:
% sleeves, openings, collar, fit, fabric, hem (the notes and Explanatory Notes
% define them that way). The facts a scenario gives about a garment:
%   the kind of G is K                      (a kind listed below)
%   G is an upper body garment              (a top: not a jacket or a coat)
%   the fabric construction of G is knitted | woven | nonwoven
%   G contains N percent of M               (M: a material listed below)
%   the cut of G indicates men | women;  G closes left over right / right over left
%   G is sized for babies                   (body height 86 cm or less, note 6/5)
%   G is made up of fabric of heading H     (5602, 5603, 5903, 5906 or 5907)
%   the components of S are matching        (a set: same fabric, colour, style, size)
%   G has at least four panels in its outer shell   (a suit jacket, note 3(a))
% and the features of tops, hosiery, gloves and scarves declared below.
%
% Provenance. Every rule is labelled and cites the passage it encodes; the
% documents say where they are published and where their text is (sources/),
% so the editor shows each passage and the verifier checks every quotation.

the target language is: prolog.

